<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <!-- 19041 is the SDK's floor for WinUI 3; SupportedOSPlatformVersion is what
         sets the MINIMUM the app runs on, and the plan asks for Windows 10 1809
         (build 17763). The Windows App SDK's own floor is 17763 for an
         unpackaged app, so the two lines differ on purpose: the first is what we
         COMPILE against, the second what we RUN on. Do not "tidy" them together. -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.17763.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>10.0.17763.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <RootNamespace>Parfast.App</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <!-- NO <Platforms> LIST, AND A DEFAULT RID. Both halves are load-bearing and
         the first real CI build is what proved it (NETSDK1032, "the
         RuntimeIdentifier platform win-x64 and the PlatformTarget x86 must be
         compatible").

         The WinUI template's `<Platforms>x86;x64;ARM64</Platforms>` does not
         include AnyCPU, and the solution asks every project for `Any CPU`. With no
         match MSBuild falls back to the FIRST platform in the list - x86 - while
         the RuntimeIdentifiers are 64-bit only, and the two disagree. Declaring no
         platforms lets this project take AnyCPU like the other four and lets the
         RID decide the architecture, which is what actually governs a
         self-contained build.

         The RID must be SET rather than merely listed, because SelfContained
         needs one and a bare `dotnet build Parfast.sln -c Release` (which is what
         CI runs) supplies none. Conditional, so `-r win-arm64` still wins. -->
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <RuntimeIdentifier Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''">win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>

    <!-- AND THE PLATFORM TARGET SET FROM THE RID, which is the half that actually
         fixed it. Removing the <Platforms> list was necessary and NOT sufficient:
         the second CI build failed with the same NETSDK1032, because the x86 does
         not come from that list at all - the Windows App SDK's own props default
         PlatformTarget to x86 when Platform is AnyCPU, which is what a solution
         built with no platform gives them. Stating it from the RID removes the
         disagreement at its source instead of guessing at who set it.
         Evaluated after RuntimeIdentifier above, so these conditions see it. -->
    <PlatformTarget Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-x64'">x64</PlatformTarget>
    <PlatformTarget Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-arm64'">ARM64</PlatformTarget>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <!-- UNPACKAGED and SELF-CONTAINED, per plan 6.2: it ships as a zip and an
         Inno Setup installer like nzbfast, not as an MSIX. WindowsPackageType
         None is what lets it run without an identity, and
         WindowsAppSDKSelfContained is what stops it needing the Windows App SDK
         runtime installed on the user's machine. Both are required; either alone
         produces an app that starts on the build box and not on a clean one. -->
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>
    <WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>true</WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <!-- EnableMsixTooling=true ON AN APP THAT SHIPS NO MSIX, WHICH IS NOT THE
         contradiction it reads as. This property was `false` until 12 Sep 2026,
         on the reasonable-looking grounds that this app ships as a zip and an
         installer - and it is the single line that made the project unbuildable
         everywhere, by everyone, including CI, for the whole of the day it
         landed.

         What the property actually selects is WHICH RESOURCE (PRI) TOOLING the
         Windows App SDK hands the project, not whether a package is produced:

           * not true -> the legacy MrtCore.PriGen path, whose UsingTask
             declarations point at
             $(MSBuildExtensionsPath)\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v$(VisualStudioVersion)\AppxPackage\,
             a Visual Studio MSBuild directory that does not exist under
             `dotnet build` in ANY SDK version. Hence, on every push since the
             app landed:

               MrtCore.PriGen.targets(914,5): error MSB4062: the
               "Microsoft.Build.Packaging.Pri.Tasks.ExpandPriContent" task could
               not be loaded from ...\sdk\<ver>\Microsoft\VisualStudio\v17.0\AppxPackage\
               Microsoft.Build.Packaging.Pri.Tasks.dll

             It was not a missing component and not one box: the GitHub runner
             fails identically on SDK 10.0.400 with v18.0 in the path, and the
             `windows` CI job was reported as having compiled this app while
             failing on exactly this.

           * true -> Microsoft.Build.Msix.Pri.targets, whose tasks live INSIDE
             the NuGet package, so they are present wherever the package
             restored. Those targets turn the legacy path off themselves, with a
             comment in Microsoft.Build.Msix.Common.props saying the two are
             incompatible.

         The packaging half stays off regardless, which is what makes this safe:
         WindowsPackageType is None above, so the app is unpackaged and has no
         identity, and nothing here produces or requires an .msix or a
         Package.appxmanifest.

         AND IT HAS TO BE THE RESOURCE TOOLING RATHER THAN NO RESOURCE TOOLING,
         which a build verdict cannot tell you. Suppressing the legacy path
         instead - either -p:AppxGeneratePriEnabled=false or
         EnableCoreMrtTooling=false - BUILDS GREEN and produces an executable
         that dies four seconds in with 0xc000027b (a stowed WinRT exception)
         inside Microsoft.UI.Xaml.dll, because compiled XAML is addressed as
         ms-appx:/// and resolved through MRT, so an app with no resources.pri
         cannot load its own App.xaml. Both were tried on 12 Sep 2026 and both
         are green-build, dead-app. If this property is ever changed, RUN the
         app before believing the build. -->
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <PublishReadyToRun>false</PublishReadyToRun>
    <AssemblyName>parfast-gui</AssemblyName>
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\parfast.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <DefaultLanguage>en-US</DefaultLanguage>
    <!-- The XAML compiler emits code this solution's TreatWarningsAsErrors would
         otherwise fail on, and it is generated code nobody here can fix.
         Narrowed to the specific numbers rather than turning the setting off. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS8305;CA1416;MSB3277</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="1.6.250228001" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="10.0.26100.1742" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parfast.Core\Parfast.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parfast.ViewModels\Parfast.ViewModels.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- NO EXPLICIT PRIResource ITEM FOR THE GENERATED COPY TABLE, and it is
         not an omission. The table is
         Parfast.App/Strings/en-US/Resources.resw, written by Parfast.Gen from
         apps/parfast/shared/strings/en.json, and x:Uid resolves against it.
         The .NET SDK already globs **/*.resw into PRIResource by default, so
         naming it here as well is the duplicate NETSDK1022 refuses:

           error NETSDK1022: Duplicate 'PRIResource' items were included ...
           The duplicate items were: 'Strings\en-US\Resources.resw'

         That was invisible while the legacy resource tooling was selected,
         because the build never got as far as collecting these items - see the
         EnableMsixTooling note above. Add EnableDefaultPriItems=false if this
         ever needs to be listed by hand; do not list it twice. -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The cdylib from parfast-ffi, copied beside the exe if it has been built.

       FIRST MATCH WINS, picked by a property rather than by a conditional item.
       The obvious spelling - one <None Include="@(Candidate)"
       Condition="Exists('%(Identity)')" /> - is MSB4190: built-in metadata is not
       allowed in a condition in an ItemGroup outside a target. That error is what
       the first CI build of this tree returned, and it is the whole content of
       this comment because the spelling looks right and is not.

       The order below is the search order. The first two are where decision D2a
       puts the artefacts - the crates are in a DETACHED cargo workspace at
       apps/parfast/Cargo.toml, so they land in apps/parfast/target/, which is what
       .github/workflows/parfast-gui.yml's windows job builds and names. The
       root-target paths are a fallback for a local build made before D2a landed.

       Conditional, never hard: the app builds and runs on MockCore when the
       library is absent, and a missing file must not fail the build. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ParfastFfiDllSearch1>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\target\release\parfast_ffi.dll</ParfastFfiDllSearch1>
    <ParfastFfiDllSearch2>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\target\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\release\parfast_ffi.dll</ParfastFfiDllSearch2>
    <!-- The mingw cross-build, which is what a Mac produces for Windows and what
         packaging/build-parfast-bundles.sh already uses for the CLI. -->
    <ParfastFfiDllSearch2b>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\target\x86_64-pc-windows-gnu\release\parfast_ffi.dll</ParfastFfiDllSearch2b>
    <ParfastFfiDllSearch3>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\..\target\release\parfast_ffi.dll</ParfastFfiDllSearch3>
    <ParfastFfiDllSearch4>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\..\target\x86_64-pc-windows-gnu\release\parfast_ffi.dll</ParfastFfiDllSearch4>

    <ParfastFfiDll Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == '' And Exists('$(ParfastFfiDllSearch1)')">$(ParfastFfiDllSearch1)</ParfastFfiDll>
    <ParfastFfiDll Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == '' And Exists('$(ParfastFfiDllSearch2)')">$(ParfastFfiDllSearch2)</ParfastFfiDll>
    <ParfastFfiDll Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == '' And Exists('$(ParfastFfiDllSearch2b)')">$(ParfastFfiDllSearch2b)</ParfastFfiDll>
    <ParfastFfiDll Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == '' And Exists('$(ParfastFfiDllSearch3)')">$(ParfastFfiDllSearch3)</ParfastFfiDll>
    <ParfastFfiDll Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == '' And Exists('$(ParfastFfiDllSearch4)')">$(ParfastFfiDllSearch4)</ParfastFfiDll>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' != ''">
    <None Include="$(ParfastFfiDll)">
      <Link>parfast_ffi.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="ParfastFfiReport" BeforeTargets="Build">
    <!-- Say which core this build will use. A silent fallback to the mock is the
         one thing about this app that must never be a surprise. -->
    <Message Importance="high" Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' != ''"
             Text="parfast: engine library found at $(ParfastFfiDll)" />
    <Message Importance="high" Condition="'$(ParfastFfiDll)' == ''"
             Text="parfast: no parfast_ffi.dll found; this build runs on MockCore." />
  </Target>
</Project>
